  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Greeting people",
     "texto": [
      "**Olá** works with everyone.",
      "Depending on the time: **bom dia** (until lunchtime), **boa tarde** (until it gets dark) and **boa noite** (at night, also as a greeting).",
      "To say goodbye: **adeus** or **até logo** (see you later)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Informal and formal «you» and other forms of address",
     "texto": [
      "**Tu** is used with people you know well; where it is used also varies by region and relationship.",
      "For formal address, **o senhor** and **a senhora** are clear options, and the verb goes in the third person.",
      "**Você** is used in Portugal, but its social value depends on the area and the context; it is not always a neutral equivalent of formal «you». If you are unsure, you can use **o senhor / a senhora** or a polite question without an explicit pronoun.",
      "To say your name: **Chamo-me Ana** or **Eu sou a Ana**. In Portugal it is very common to use the article with first names in familiar speech (**o Pedro, a Ana**), but it is not compulsory: **Sou Ana** is also correct.",
      "To ask it: **Como te chamas?** (informal) or **Como se chama?** (formal)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Thank you",
     "texto": [
      "«Thank you» changes depending on who is speaking: a man says **obrigado** and a woman says **obrigada**.",
      "When meeting someone: **muito prazer** (pleased to meet you)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
